#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605} :activity/id #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176968 :tutorial/content "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" :step/math-academy-id 12791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175768 :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" :step/math-academy-id 12792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522705}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" :step/math-academy-id 12794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" :step/math-academy-id 12795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177036 :tutorial/content "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5745 :tutorial/title "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" :step/math-academy-id 35242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176121}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010} :activity/id #uuid "b940a17e-dd53-53d8-bf19-3b3ed1a866fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2fb7032-76c6-5840-a4b3-75b3bca0c64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468022} {:answer/id #uuid "7da58ced-39e0-5469-8345-6d5c5c71fd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5aeadf-4637-56d4-8985-6b916361fe7c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b8f35-5eb0-5d60-99e3-d78e15538715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468024} {:answer/id #uuid "87c1ca6e-9c20-5182-95f4-352e35d95fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468024} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b25706e-95ef-5012-a511-a60525ecadd8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4850f6e7-44e4-5c2c-ab1c-1fd4345196e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468026} {:answer/id #uuid "5d89fdc2-6b87-505d-9741-6111f42f5c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468027} :answer-field/id #uuid "bafe51e4-c08a-55a8-b1fd-0a9ee0fb0ef6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" :question/math-academy-id "q-73484" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\nI. $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc37e5f-628a-5453-b9aa-5f7736bbc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} {:answer/id #uuid "86c33542-1a37-5a8c-829b-f0ce4c00b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c6904f-d234-579f-a1ce-7aceea241e6b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b766daf9-f662-5b41-bf41-146b6ff13df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467592} {:answer/id #uuid "ffeccce2-717e-5e80-b3c0-db1259995cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593} :answer-field/id #uuid "814f8eea-219e-55ac-a376-98a938bdbd22" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7babf862-7892-5b69-9b8c-2026a9d0a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} {:answer/id #uuid "61ed1c67-3d18-527d-aa8b-5e81455211be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cc6ec8-9bed-5ab7-9e0d-2ce973417c1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a410e5f1-db4f-5d99-8a2f-cd16595bdd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-47923" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $[0.5,1]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08258f8b-dcaa-5fc6-ae65-f632b2ec32c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} {:answer/id #uuid "29791dee-07cd-517e-ade1-ff8025ea2d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} :answer-field/id #uuid "e380e8d8-76cf-5f78-bbc9-d5f2bfff507e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fa6caaf-0d53-550a-81a3-65d8c87e7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} {:answer/id #uuid "7b92b7c9-5e55-5053-bc30-ae2124f51f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7306f5-05ce-59d5-926c-66b49d14119f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ffb37d-c85f-5070-8a42-00349cde98df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468056} {:answer/id #uuid "332ea8dc-cd67-5380-9b7b-ad3430d95bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acf4f19-1ed8-5841-bd74-4e01d898c416" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $(0,3]$\n- **III.** $[-2,-1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508480} {:answer/id #uuid "690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508548} {:answer/id #uuid "7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508576} {:answer/id #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131070" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e532f7-a8d4-5ad0-87fa-e0abb07a53ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} {:answer/id #uuid "166b32ef-7d9c-5749-b3c9-7228a4faa2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} :answer-field/id #uuid "11346c34-25dc-5818-adc6-68489744c4a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468726} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcab39e9-589e-5993-9821-bf3f565fc3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468012} {:answer/id #uuid "5faf65eb-1420-5b19-84fa-7b4d080f51e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468013} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd1c1baa-2550-538a-9bce-29f2173a21b4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137588e4-2424-5d62-8fe6-5492472a9566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468014} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6eb95e-2a8c-5ac0-b216-c72fc2f4b9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468015} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cf26ba3-fa7e-5c2b-86e3-65a942cdda6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "122f233b-5382-5fef-8fbd-2b8682266e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-73481" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509003}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508384}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065206} :activity/id #uuid "4dd7be6e-e9a7-53a7-9076-0bf7c12d09ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048919 :topic/id #uuid "cc7fb2dc-62dc-56f4-976f-30935824115a" :topic/math-academy-id 344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065396 :tutorial/content "When a function $y=y(x)$ contains complex products, quotients, or radicals, we can use logarithmic differentiation to find the derivative $y'(x).$ \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\ny = x^2 \\cdot 2^x\n$$\n\n \n\nOur goal is to find $y'(x).$ To do this, we will use the product rule for logarithms, namely\n\n$$\n\\ln(a b)= \\ln a + \\ln b.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe differentiate our function by following four steps.\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the product and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Next, we differentiate both sides of our equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}+(x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Then, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y =x^2\\cdot 2^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}(\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {2}^{x+1}+{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1895a7f3-5ba4-5a31-85b3-6a7b050b307c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6088 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "caef414d-4f2a-5504-9b92-39a03d2f3c8f" :step/math-academy-id 37035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065207}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064146 :question/id #uuid "c24da7ef-0070-5aae-bade-c82ea56476ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9845"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506581}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"} :step/id #uuid "b6a516b4-d0c4-5ec7-8f7e-12edd0e85c51" :step/math-academy-id 37036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065210}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064147 :question/id #uuid "7db11c8c-344b-5d87-a8ae-3b006e5bd92b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9846"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050542}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534090}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"} :step/id #uuid "57a60273-bc58-5aff-8dcd-d7139782aa2a" :step/math-academy-id 37037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064148 :question/id #uuid "e0cec84d-96cf-5fed-ba05-a5cfc57841a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9847"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050894}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554576}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"} :step/id #uuid "8850f22e-33ee-545a-8f5e-4dc37bda3b30" :step/math-academy-id 37038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065210 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065397 :tutorial/content "In cases where we wish to differentiate a quotient of functions\n\n$$\ny =  f(x) = \\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)},\n$$\n\nwe can apply the quotient rule for logarithms: \n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, let's compute $y',$ where the function $y$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e^{x^2}}{(1-2x)^2(x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{2}(x-1)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-2x)^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x+\\frac{4}{1-2x}-\\frac{3}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x+\\frac{4}{1-2x}-\\frac{3}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute our expression for $y,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{2}(x-1)^{3}}(2x+\\frac{4}{1-2x}-\\frac{3}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{2}(x-1)^{3}}(\\frac{2x(1-2x)(x-1)+4(x-1)-3(1-2x)}{(1-2x)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}(2x(1-2x)(x-1)+4(x-1)-3(1-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}(-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+8x-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-8x+7)}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8cf6e625-669a-5fda-b756-e1ce091eaa93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6090 :tutorial/title "Using Logarithms to Differentiate a Quotient"} :step/id #uuid "2141558f-a6d2-515d-902a-620581d72401" :step/math-academy-id 37041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065208}}] :activity/title "Further Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064435}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554696} :activity/id #uuid "93bbf55d-a1b3-5160-aa13-5dae73451322" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048919 :topic/id #uuid "cc7fb2dc-62dc-56f4-976f-30935824115a" :topic/math-academy-id 344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c61cfcf-a787-550b-810a-38a9ae04f7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533208} {:answer/id #uuid "9411358e-dbc0-567a-a1c3-e8fae0dff5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533497} {:answer/id #uuid "96e9100a-3748-5dd5-ad4e-9a90375f9d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533508} {:answer/id #uuid "c744d1de-7757-5a26-ac48-bc584e98ec52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533617} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c60272-837e-5905-b5bf-1a14841fc162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533508} :answer-field/id #uuid "52f2bc75-08e1-5592-986f-bf5ac47ce09a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc13fb5c-5303-5c81-bafe-333779ced9ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-138854" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(g(x)-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{3/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}(\\frac{6x-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}(\\frac{6x-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6x-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=6x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ba10ec7-4252-52ba-8bc0-ceb77d7dadf1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80386a1f-1c68-542d-97ab-e6ec02fe80f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt[3]{(x+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554125} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb339eb-1cc4-5d35-9e83-6c06760cd1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(3x+6)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554219} {:answer/id #uuid "c88b7035-072c-5214-bd55-1804a6aa0ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(3x+6)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554310} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ae8cb1-1ad1-519a-a9ae-fc0f9b33b01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{3x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554361} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4a59e0-949a-5cc8-91d2-09ecb393925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{(x+2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554310} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c497ffd-4cca-50b5-a7fc-dc93d720ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c789989-28b9-52e0-ac72-a0f8b7d66a60" :question/math-academy-id "q-138860" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}}{\\sqrt[3]{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6/3}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2)^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3x+6)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3x+6}\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x(3x+6)-({x}^{2}+2)}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{11{x}^{2}+24x-2}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "73bd8f64-f826-5721-8dca-5f9a8f22b62d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f947482-c2c6-568d-82f0-29af6bfba254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}+x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533253} {:answer/id #uuid "3e350aae-2cd2-5f81-8e5d-74dec200744b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}+x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533284} {:answer/id #uuid "67b07a11-17f0-5c4c-a4ca-8e59d532b7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533382} {:answer/id #uuid "89588797-26b1-5b42-8b21-2029da27dbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+2x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533468} {:answer/id #uuid "b35826d5-c07d-51fb-931e-4b0d6ab4dd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+2x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533382} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d94c7c1-de0e-5fb5-af87-71126c21e380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "deb7ada4-14d9-5457-acd5-ef77477a4e36" :question/math-academy-id "q-138832" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}g(x)}{(x+8)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{4}+2x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{4}+2x-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+2x-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4{x}^{3}+2-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =4{x}^{3}+2-\\frac{6}{x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(4{x}^{3}+2-\\frac{6}{x+8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}(4{x}^{3}+2-\\frac{6}{x+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}(\\frac{(4{x}^{3}+2)(x+8)-6}{x+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{7}}((4{x}^{3}+2)(x+8)-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{7}}(4{x}^{4}+32{x}^{3}+2x+16-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}(2{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+x+5)}{(x+8)^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+x+5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85cfab1f-9554-509a-b407-c56fc29d97eb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e00fa1-2d53-53c2-91c2-dbaf97414222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533296} {:answer/id #uuid "84e65e07-d407-5744-b09c-1d7419c5f733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533452} {:answer/id #uuid "949b2307-7470-54c1-a231-4a7923ae08c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533499} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5538e4-cb5c-53d2-8282-324285197339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533525} {:answer/id #uuid "cffa59f6-f8b3-5069-84a6-83cd2e84beae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533296} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81d8766-b273-5742-81e1-135f6f4971d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3859b1c-a23b-517a-bfe5-a836f749be10" :question/math-academy-id "q-138834" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x-1}}{(1-3x)^{6}}g(x)+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-3x)^{5}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2x)-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-3x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x})+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\frac{2{x}^{2}(1-3x)+15({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(1-3x)})+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\frac{17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15}{({x}^{2}+1)(1-3x)})+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x-1}}{(1-3x)^{6}}(17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15)+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96a56253-16ed-52e2-abe6-273309cfe7a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c406db1-ac6c-5309-b97f-14fd566c17b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x^{2}+x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533391} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d713dab-487c-57b4-848a-8f859a772276" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88fe2774-53cf-5c90-a007-5fb6a5d78b33" :question/math-academy-id "q-343529" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[$\n{{field-1}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-({x}^{2}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-(2x+3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{5}{x-2}-(2x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[\\frac{5}{x-2}-(2x+3)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[\\frac{5}{x-2}-(2x+3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[\\frac{5-(x-2)(2x+3)}{x-2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[5-(x-2)(2x+3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[5-(2{x}^{2}-x-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[11+x-2{x}^{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d420e20c-8165-5de9-8370-feb30c2da60d"}] :activity/title "Further Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553850}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176793} :activity/id #uuid "bf0ecc39-1fe8-504f-aa46-f80a00074638" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049325 :topic/id #uuid "ec0cc1a2-4f65-5bb2-b5ae-116b4850cf11" :topic/math-academy-id 3671} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177028 :tutorial/content "Recall that Euler's number can be defined as the following special limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{m \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{m}\\right)^{m}\n$$\n\nWe can derive an alternative version of this limit by making the substitution\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac 1m\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that if $m\\to \\infty$ then $n \\to 0$. Therefore, applying our substitution, we arrive at the following important result:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+{n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "490157c8-33d8-5f0d-ad6e-f837c839499f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef158b4c-de98-5033-958d-aa50b93d6909" :step/math-academy-id 35058 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175873 :question/id #uuid "1cc5d11a-c497-5d8f-84b4-9cbef561db28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c8893eb-cc9c-59b1-98c8-ec326a352e28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049769}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176308}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using the Alternative Limit Definition of Euler's Number"} :step/id #uuid "ac8ee34c-7875-5657-81c3-a58946bcf638" :step/math-academy-id 35060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176019 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175874 :question/id #uuid "caad75a3-4ee4-5038-b9e1-5e296d83974a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dbad5d4-6001-5be5-9ed3-0b959f4d7d92" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508979}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "797a10b6-090b-55e9-8c36-939dfb954adc" :step/math-academy-id 35066 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177029 :tutorial/content "Let's take stock of our two special limit definitions of Euler's number:\n\n- $\\displaystyle e = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}$\n- $\\displaystyle e = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{1/n}$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate more complex limits. For example, suppose we wish to evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{x+3}\\right)^{x+3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $x \\to \\infty$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{x+3}\\right)^{x+3}} = 1^\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^n = e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x+3}=\\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x + 3} &= \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\nx + 3 &= 2n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)(1 + \\frac{2}{x + 3})^{x + 3} &= lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{2n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)[(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39210b04-2951-5b70-adbb-7a6690671dac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5710 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit That Resembles a Special Limit Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "129e19b0-cbae-5677-bfcb-93c996ee597b" :step/math-academy-id 35067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176022 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175877 :question/id #uuid "63c2e3b3-3418-5f74-8ebc-34af9fd6bf56" :question/math-academy-id "e-9377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9308a83f-ed86-5cbc-9b23-c1fec9fca6a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508976}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using a Variable Substitution: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "88bbb3ae-6920-5592-b9b2-5833516142b8" :step/math-academy-id 35109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177032 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(1-\\sin{x}\\right)^{3/\\sin{x}}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $x \\to 0$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(1-\\sin{x}\\right)^{3/\\sin{x}} = 1^{\\infty}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{1/n} = e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\sin x)^{3/\\sin x} &= lim_(x → 0)(1 + (-\\sin x))^{3/\\sin x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $-\\sin{x}=n$. Therefore, since $n \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\sin x)^{3/\\sin x} &= lim_(x → 0)(1 + (-\\sin x))^{3/\\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{3/(-n)} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)[(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{-3} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{-3} \\\\\n&= e^{-3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{e^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fdccc8f6-9726-5fd5-846c-74c2c5b1ed28" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5717 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit Containing a Minus Sign Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "68a5d41c-4aef-5d14-b412-4042829e22c7" :step/math-academy-id 35113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177035 :tutorial/content "We have shown that Euler's number $e$ is given by the following limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+{n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$\n\nLet's use this result to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{2/n}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $n \\to 0$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{2/n}} = 1^\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nHowever, by rewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and then applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{2/n} &= lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{2 \\cdot 1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)[(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cde764e2-c036-59de-af59-f13454800e69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5722 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit Using the Alternative Limit Definition of Euler's Number"} :step/id #uuid "1a2ed7d1-2776-5a8c-9da9-bd1e0c7a4cdc" :step/math-academy-id 35122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176794}}] :activity/title "Further Limits Involving the Exponential Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176173}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509014} :activity/id #uuid "f2d15eff-4bf3-528d-a472-58ed556f58f2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049325 :topic/id #uuid "ec0cc1a2-4f65-5bb2-b5ae-116b4850cf11" :topic/math-academy-id 3671} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141e356a-c0b9-53fb-a3fa-1c304d6a2600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508418} {:answer/id #uuid "24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508448} {:answer/id #uuid "35e61fc0-40f6-5aef-922f-50f62512b14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508469} {:answer/id #uuid "5e34251a-f4f3-549b-a7a0-aeaadf3cbdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508531} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfcba74-2e37-531b-b078-1a79a99a831c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508448} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac530e0e-d3d1-5a9a-9415-9ac86edc2ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-118714" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3145e340-cda7-5adf-9900-0d495fc4e789" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc1ba37-52fc-50dc-b984-9dc568bfe31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508478} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508481} {:answer/id #uuid "7affb6d5-dcf4-5d5f-997f-81bf1706c88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508570} {:answer/id #uuid "fdebe404-8aed-5769-8c60-015f60d99e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508806} {:answer/id #uuid "fef041c0-b3ba-511a-a1db-5120dc4f98a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508481} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e72dc91-9bb2-5a52-9ea8-c8a3392b078f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" :question/math-academy-id "q-118712" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1})}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a3949a3-5808-5f9d-a44a-dabfca5b2e15" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1642b7-884e-5096-ad66-1a3b13f3c305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508623} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb677ded-facb-5ee5-9acc-a1da685a4a6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cff12c80-2e8b-52fc-a40b-42f5ea296e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338566" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n} \\\\ x & =3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2(3n)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{6} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99de8130-883e-53ab-99b6-1a108d294a59"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd2332-d9e4-5f07-a6f6-b1f461806d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508431} {:answer/id #uuid "31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508463} {:answer/id #uuid "edae2223-31c8-57b8-91a2-c2b71c6e6090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508771} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b08025-f86f-5c45-b65e-e33d0d2c521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508795} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b94c54-bdfd-5cd4-a505-1c37eeea500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508463} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bcd67e-475b-5366-91b7-b32feba0f989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-118721" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(2x-2)+1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $2(x-1)=n$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) & =n \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b6ed0ab8-eebb-5a58-85d6-828a7ea195c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142517b8-196c-572d-bb30-b593804d312b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508419} {:answer/id #uuid "2e13745f-0660-51aa-b3c9-308fbcc11a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508459} {:answer/id #uuid "b79e0202-007e-5255-baf6-8ab1013d2835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508665} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fa933c-70d4-5eac-8738-8875677c238c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508726} {:answer/id #uuid "eab3a712-8119-5c80-9935-77d99f9b65c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508419} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d49d40-4bea-500d-bdf8-019158dcd1ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91b96bb8-d0ec-5384-8420-38fd513a4a78" :question/math-academy-id "q-127543" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $t\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $3t-6=n.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t-6 & =n \\\\ 3(t-2) & =n \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{n}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c4026a05-5a37-59aa-a6fd-1b0c2c9aa547" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509025}}] :activity/title "Further Limits Involving the Exponential Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508387}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097774} :activity/id #uuid "acc24286-334e-50b7-b51f-39796fa9c946" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521 :topic/id #uuid "5036af57-460d-5886-bb25-967e885ae634" :topic/math-academy-id 1140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097927 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.\n$$\n\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n\n$$\n\\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe usually let $u = x,$ but that would leave us with $v' = \\ln(x)$ which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our $u$ and $v'$ slightly differently, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{u}\\cdot {{x}_{⏟}}_{{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should *always* identify the logarithmic function with $u,$ not with $v'.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbf22d18-c638-527f-a085-8fcc19fbfbeb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 897 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5d3fd98-fbd8-5b6d-a250-876573acf24e" :step/math-academy-id 618388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096475 :question/id #uuid "c7373a16-c90a-5875-8b17-8724542b35ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31a28451-005e-50dc-a7cf-5b666b1d7735" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487357}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Natural Logarithm"} :step/id #uuid "333f87c0-71a4-538e-9783-f364abf0caa7" :step/math-academy-id 618389 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096476 :question/id #uuid "672d52f6-89d8-550d-863b-7c5157d59ca3" :question/math-academy-id "e-494"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487393}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"} :step/id #uuid "fdfdcd42-ada8-5eea-ae27-e68cd87d7470" :step/math-academy-id 618392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096427 :question/id #uuid "12811582-7156-5b84-9ba9-5545f87894f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3993"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487389}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor"} :step/id #uuid "c0d6168f-5f91-5b54-9679-17a077751add" :step/math-academy-id 618395}] :activity/title "Using Integration by Parts to Calculate Integrals With Logarithms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096910}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055571} :activity/id #uuid "95aa7191-773d-555a-b024-bb0d165f5cd9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050540 :topic/id #uuid "521360f4-4398-5d66-ae61-73dcd0f74f49" :topic/math-academy-id 51} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056045 :tutorial/content "Suppose $S$ is a set. The **power set** of $S,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(S),$ is the set containing every subset of $S,$ *including* the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the power set of the set\n\n$$\nS = \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we list out all the subsets of $S{:}$\n\n- The subset containing no elements is $\\emptyset.$\n\n- The subsets containing $1$ element are $\\{ 1 \\},$ $\\{ 2 \\},$ and $\\{ 3 \\}.$\n\n- The subsets containing $2$ elements are $\\{ 1,2 \\},$  $\\{ 1,3 \\},$ and  $\\{ 2,3 \\}.$\n\n- The subset containing $3$ elements is $S$ itself: $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$\n\nNow, let's put all these subsets together as elements of a single set. We get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\emptyset, \\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{ 1,2 \\}, \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\{ 2,3 \\}, \\{ 1,2,3 \\} \\big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cbbf2155-81d5-52d9-b9c0-2416958838b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8241 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cccf59fe-5d64-567d-835c-def5b2fb5c93" :step/math-academy-id 153365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056060 :tutorial/content "Another way to generate all the subsets of $S=\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is using a tree diagram. We start off with the empty set $\\{\\}$ and generate additional subsets of $S$ by systematically inserting or excluding elements of $S.$\n\nLet's go through the process.\n\n- Start with the empty set $\\{\\}$ at the root node of our tree. This is the parent node.\n\n- Draw two branches pointing away from the parent node. The tip of each arrow gives a child node. Label one branch 'No' and the other 'Yes'.\n\n![](images/a4/a4b17d8114cc48cdc858d036b839d766dcb60f3b400a096406479c9daf7afac0.png)\n\n- We'll now write down a subset of $S$ at each child node:\n\n- At the child node (tip of the arrow) whose branch is labeled 'No,' we write down the parent node's set.\n\n$$\n\\{\\}\n$$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *first* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{1\\}\n$$\n\nWe now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/61/61c6c192df26a22cac5dddad6b2692a519da055730fe9738f3173cb38229cf8b.png)\n\nWe repeat the process, setting the subsets found in the previous step as the new parent nodes. \n\n- We start by considering the parent node $\\{\\}{:}$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'No', we write down the parent node's set.\n\n$$\n\\{\\}\n$$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *second* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{2\\}\n$$\n\nWe now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6e/6e43b6022b5939b824995936d8ab910b7b3d2e821a6c96be63606245f6e60dbb.png)\n\n- Then, we consider the parent node $\\{1\\}{:}$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'No', we write down the parent node's set.\n\n$$\n\\{1\\}\n$$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *second* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{1,2\\}\n$$\n\nWe now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6f/6f81c80039f14260bdfdb8d55a34820874fe518e076d2718629d4faf385e92d5.png)\n\n- Finally, we repeat the process for the new parent nodes $\\{\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{1\\},$ and $\\{1,2\\}.$ \n\nOur final tree diagram looks as follows.\n\n![](images/12/127ee1187ebf3a2cc498c88326285d5adf5d609cf3276885e8c9d525435133eb.png)\n\nThe final column lists all subsets of $S.$ \n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\},\\quad \\{1\\},\\quad \\{2\\},\\quad \\{3\\},\\quad \\{ 1,2 \\},\\quad \\{ 1,3 \\},\\quad \\{ 2,3 \\},\\quad \\{ 1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nFinally then, we can write down $\\mathcal P(S){:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\emptyset, \\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{ 1,2 \\}, \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\{ 2,3 \\}, \\{ 1,2,3 \\} \\big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a2897eb-b185-5619-bc49-f2ef12df345a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8511 :tutorial/title "Using a Tree Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "72c3927c-894d-55ae-8094-79014856a6ad" :step/math-academy-id 153366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054637 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054446 :question/id #uuid "a138e1a7-52c5-5763-975e-cab082266099" :question/math-academy-id "e-13199"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f1f99d3-ebcb-50c6-a162-bd58f7d5ec92" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495098}] :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements in a Power Set"} :step/id #uuid "ba17ca05-c9c8-5cb0-9680-49c6db006781" :step/math-academy-id 153367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056046 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a finite set $S$ with cardinality $|S|.$ Then, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(S)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a finite set with cardinality $|S|$ has $2^{|S|}$ subsets.\n\nFor example, for the set $S = \\{ a,b,c \\},$ we have $|S| = 3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|} = 2^3 = 8.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that $S$ has $8$ subsets, and consequently, $\\mathcal{P}(S)$ has $8$ elements. \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{\n\\underbrace{\\emptyset}_1,\n\\underbrace{ \\{a\\}}_2,\n\\underbrace{ \\{b\\}}_3,\n\\underbrace{ \\{c\\}}_4,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,b \\}}_5,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,c \\}}_6,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ b,c \\}}_7,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,b,c \\}}_8 \n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- It's clear from our rule that if $S$ is finite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is finite.\n\n- It can be shown that if $S$ is infinite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is also infinite.\n\nWe'll discuss power sets of infinite sets at the end of this lesson and in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "fa764524-a501-5804-b1a1-6523e739b234" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8242 :tutorial/title "The Cardinality of a Power Set"} :step/id #uuid "d9225952-f921-5ed9-a6b9-726af08be8b6" :step/math-academy-id 153371 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054638 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054447 :question/id #uuid "a72a78b7-eaef-59a3-a23a-82d291e8253a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13200"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e22a7b0-753b-51b1-8946-5d17456aa778" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516764}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Power Set"} :step/id #uuid "5f5b95d2-66ba-5245-bd79-47140cb7833a" :step/math-academy-id 153372 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054448 :question/id #uuid "b8db07f6-cdbf-5d00-8f3c-6f8b07277ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13201"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ec1dc0-4e21-5001-959e-ed35039ca00e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495126}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cardinality in More Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "d77da74f-73f3-59f5-9290-13740989504d" :step/math-academy-id 153376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054449 :question/id #uuid "ceb40e91-12d8-5636-8b98-fa41aaca33b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13214"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495138}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"} :step/id #uuid "3544c1c7-cd8e-5b1d-a95a-e07e977f3121" :step/math-academy-id 153379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056061 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, our primary focus was on power sets of *finite* sets. The definition of the power set of an *infinite* set mirrors that of finite sets. Namely, it comprises all possible subsets.\n\nAs we already understand, for finite sets, the size of power sets grows exponentially based on the size of the underlying set: \n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(X) \\big| = 2^{|X|}\n$$\n\nWhen dealing with power sets of infinite sets, the situation is more intricate. \n\nFor example, let's consider $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ the set containing all points on the Cartesian plane. What are the elements of the power set $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb R^2)?$\n\nAny collection of points on the plane is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ Such subsets can be visualized as black-and-white images on the plane, where points belonging to the subset are drawn in black, and points not belonging to the subset are represented in white.\n\nFor instance, each of the following images gives us a specific element of $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^2){:}$\n\n![](images/bb/bbdb49640183563e0cb137b3c57095edd8b81bceac4dcda1bf3460371df17930.png)\n\nIt follows that any text, be it any or all existing Math Academy lessons, those planned for the future, or any other possible text (finite or infinite), including those not yet written, are all contained within $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^2).$ \n\n![](images/c4/c47487cf1a7253bfafd5419cd824df30669a575d4b90f3fddc36566e920325bc.png)\n\nIt's somewhat extraordinary that such a vast and important set can be expressed using just the symbols $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb R^2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca3f303c-7c55-5abe-945a-dfe8a4816aa8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8512 :tutorial/title "Power Sets of Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "3f3fd95b-66c4-5c77-81de-9a4ba5c39f8e" :step/math-academy-id 153382}] :activity/title "Power Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054910}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516944} :activity/id #uuid "3fdc85fd-9417-5f27-878a-26fa67cced22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050540 :topic/id #uuid "521360f4-4398-5d66-ae61-73dcd0f74f49" :topic/math-academy-id 51} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bff3a4f-a797-5315-a093-a0e43bf9c1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516204} {:answer/id #uuid "4807edcf-c198-541c-af49-03cb7d0192f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516257} {:answer/id #uuid "603ac4dc-cd6a-524a-b62e-bb84d69c82a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516321} {:answer/id #uuid "e691460b-9158-5c1a-aaac-006dfe7e1086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516591} {:answer/id #uuid "fb07557b-9f6f-5930-9255-eb2d7fc0c0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516637} :answer-field/id #uuid "a26ebfcb-41a7-5e48-bdd5-7e3c756aa992" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0186524d-998b-54c6-a0f5-fa7d2edb8c89" :question/math-academy-id "q-105146" :question/problem "If $A=\\{1,8,\\{8,9\\},5,4,\\{2,7\\}\\}$, then $|P(A)|=$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $A,$ we have $|P(A)|={2}^{|A|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|A|=6,$ so we have\n\n$$\n|P(A)|={2}^{|A|}={2}^{6}=64.\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\{8,9\\}$ is a single element in the set $A,$ and the same is true for $\\{2,7\\}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0f236f0b-57a6-5471-a446-6ce42cac2342" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33df4f48-f059-5aac-b8f0-f099e31a2bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383682} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dd68b1-f65d-5184-bbb4-c555eedfe6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383683} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6f8df5-cbdf-56ae-80f0-dad4e1c2aaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383684} {:answer/id #uuid "d7be702c-cb58-5a32-b0c0-22dff292aa5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383685} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f161af-bd82-5618-b052-e3a775b0c121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383684} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a504af7-d661-5466-ae33-ffb2c3dbb73f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "081807a1-5337-5569-850c-b1949ae55f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-182221" :question/problem "If $A=\\{0\\}$ and $B=\\{5,6\\}$ then $|P(P(A))\\times B|=$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $S,$ we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ Also, $|S\\times T|=|S|\\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n|A|=1,\\,|B|=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(A)| & ={2}^{|A|}={2}^{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(A))| & ={2}^{|P(A)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(A))\\times B| & =|P(P(A))|\\cdot |B| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50dc482e-fe48-5c6f-985b-70ceb6762c4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516980}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6aaf71b-4960-5961-afa1-c9c1ddf33ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373259} {:answer/id #uuid "ba508c22-2dcf-59c2-9539-fe7661c099d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373260} {:answer/id #uuid "74e7ce6a-8798-5ed4-9de9-efd5aa83a65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "129" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373261} {:answer/id #uuid "1f14efe5-5f0a-5fee-9ee2-7e85505b55ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373262} {:answer/id #uuid "08770093-a28f-5166-ad8f-c1cf99c46184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373261} :answer-field/id #uuid "58649371-77c2-5771-ab5a-7b1cc10ddd57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b98b713-be0c-56a7-9337-a9c7958ec47d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80971" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are sets. If $|X|=0$ and $|Y|=7,$ then $|P(X)|+|P(Y)|=$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $S$, $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|X|=0$ and $|Y|=7,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(X)|+|P(Y)| & ={2}^{|X|}+{2}^{|Y|} \\\\  & ={2}^{0}+{2}^{7} \\\\  & =1+128 \\\\  & =129.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4c2be1d-d683-59b4-aa78-adac0b909f27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b6c47e-8246-50f3-9325-54dd77f76d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379129} {:answer/id #uuid "904f598d-3ed1-5d74-84dc-36bb3ad3d517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379130} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0824ad-dbf5-54af-90b1-3f90cb1373f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379131} {:answer/id #uuid "4632f7af-3aba-5c19-854c-e70ab7d1d1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379132} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb171f1-886c-5af0-989c-9c887889a747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379129} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eed9f6b-2f01-5f80-ae09-36f055b25ac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11747dae-0095-5e9a-a1d3-7c1819d5b4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-182217" :question/problem "If $|Z|=1$ then $|P(P(P(Z)))|=$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $S$, we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $|Z|=1.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n|P(Z)|={2}^{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(Z))| & ={2}^{|P(Z)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(P(Z)))| & ={2}^{|P(P(Z))|} \\\\  & ={2}^{4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5910cbe-18d6-5500-b155-fa1070c1e5b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d607d8-d309-5ca9-83bc-740b4c36bade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471191} {:answer/id #uuid "325fb8bb-d47f-5880-a8b1-b79555ac108d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471192} :answer-field/id #uuid "42d12dc6-b94a-52d8-8494-2498d1c73f28" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53559d5d-159d-5fdc-bd9d-4e94bdf5ac8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471193} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e30a64-f23f-5094-890c-8e7f4d8a8222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471193} :answer-field/id #uuid "b856be72-7e15-5ad6-945e-6975defb3028" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472102} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b52a727-ad5e-5373-9c5a-a0274dde1602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471195} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8607f7-f14a-5008-becc-2bb0c03d4f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471196} :answer-field/id #uuid "5def3004-16f9-5b6c-af86-0253fe70b521" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c162f27f-023e-512b-91b9-54632834b487" :question/math-academy-id "q-182238" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are finite?\n- **I.** $P({R,e})$\n- **II.** $P({x\\in R∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0})$\n- **III.** $P({(-1)^{n}∣n\\in Z})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({R,e})={∅,\\,{R},\\,{e},\\,{R,e}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\n- Set II is infinite since the underlying set ${x\\in R∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0}$  is infinite.\n\n- Set III is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\n{(-1)^{n}∣n\\in Z}={-1,1},\n$$\n\nand, as a result, \n\n$$\nP({(-1)^{n}∣n\\in Z})=P({-1,1})={∅,\\,{-1},\\,{1},\\,{-1,1}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ebc42d35-f4dd-5905-852b-c5cb495a2b71"}] :activity/title "Power Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516093}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079268} :activity/id #uuid "128934d0-aa66-5625-aab3-6d772fa10846" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050643 :topic/id #uuid "5ba94c26-75d7-5806-9fad-73f65947835b" :topic/math-academy-id 637} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079652 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to calculate the limit\n\n$$\nL=\\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln(2+h) -\\ln(2)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe key here is to notice that the limit looks very similar to the instantaneous rate of change formula,\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nfor the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\ln x\n$$\n\nat $a=2$.\n\nLet's substitute\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\ln x\n$$\n\nand $a=2$ into the above, just to be sure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(h → 0)(f(2 + h) - f(2))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\ln (2 + h) - \\ln (2))/(h) \\\\\n&= L\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L = f'(2)$. So instead of working out that difficult limit, we only need to work out $f'(2)$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{1}{x}, \\qquad f'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that\n\n$$\nL=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db8ca187-38da-5852-8df8-cd1cb0f754cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b7e08e0-d936-5e59-996f-679f185c3562" :step/math-academy-id 12352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078279 :question/id #uuid "5f4f75ce-d8df-5970-bffe-99beb9d9aac9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c089a-9506-5a0e-83ae-48efa8fd314c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234076}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Instantaneous Rate of Change Formula"} :step/id #uuid "cca4127e-8b00-5649-abdf-3f0477315c35" :step/math-academy-id 12353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079653 :tutorial/content "We can apply the same method for calculating limits that are similar to the equivalent definition of the derivative,\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\n$$\n\nIt is important to choose the correct form for each limit. If the numerator of the limit looks like $f(a+h) -f(a)$ for some $a$ and the denominator looks like $h$, then use the form\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nBut if the numerator looks like $f(x)-f(a)$ and the denominator looks like $x-a$ for some $a$, then use the form\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88b647b7-8adf-517b-848b-5323c6565ced" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2632 :tutorial/title "Recognizing the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative in Limits"} :step/id #uuid "a1fec940-4ec4-58c8-8553-9ddb7fd70bff" :step/math-academy-id 12354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078280 :question/id #uuid "3b5966f8-c23f-5ef1-8e5c-7862a020402d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b613bf7-a641-547f-8487-d6af0da0f724" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522712}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "4bbfd52a-c53b-5698-a9e3-0f3cadd9059f" :step/math-academy-id 12355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078281 :question/id #uuid "4a42aea1-4927-5d6e-af94-d43b834e295a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3652"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20df0d0d-a94e-55e6-8d19-25a566ab794d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507501}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits Equivalent to a Derivative Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "6d9b76a5-30f4-5ef3-8887-46953ed35bc3" :step/math-academy-id 12356}] :activity/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078660}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507581} :activity/id #uuid "52ac8d49-41b5-5bd8-bf9d-dfa1ea8ebfa8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050643 :topic/id #uuid "5ba94c26-75d7-5806-9fad-73f65947835b" :topic/math-academy-id 637} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd58159-33f5-5db7-909d-d7a72f3ebd4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7fe715dd-222c-5303-8e5d-661af680f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-314062" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "06ee9d72-c64c-59d0-a5fe-be8ba76923b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5ff7cc-8b3f-5a39-aa5b-ab6f585428d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506701} {:answer/id #uuid "11e63def-7844-5262-ab80-238e82afc4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506715} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7e125d-4a64-5403-b4f5-240ac6952b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506826} {:answer/id #uuid "7c767c64-2e2b-560b-95b1-27629324438c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506952} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94670c-c1ce-57cc-9599-b00476cfd5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506701} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b3df459-f0ab-5ba2-a113-901da7e7d2cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac305827-2b68-5246-9f72-b59f76116099" :question/math-academy-id "q-104948" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(2).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=2\\cdot 2=4,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $L=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "0ffadd5f-2484-54bc-aafa-ecea2ecea3f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb6a2c0-9b12-5d02-8114-ec93e9fd1dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506895} {:answer/id #uuid "96e83254-6e53-5553-a48a-26aa54776b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507006} {:answer/id #uuid "a27838c1-ffc6-51e6-ad62-1715c0672f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507029} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ec119b-815d-53e3-b075-5f37bb3efbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507069} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fd154a-9418-5779-9f22-3520453bf8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506895} :answer-field/id #uuid "008a3a74-eacb-51da-8605-e2fef59db043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbf6525f-a6b2-5d79-bc38-725ad8dae9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104976" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e03f453156d57af94b13b76e22dc366644b18f56acc5f9a5f4a2c005e6bec3f7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}=-1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Comparing these limits with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(0), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(1), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function consists of three line segments. Inspecting the graph, we see that\n\n- for $-4<x<-1$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n- for $-1<x<1$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$\n\n- for $1<x<4$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n The derivative does not exist at $x=\\pm 1.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & =0, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} & =\\text{DNE}, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} :step/id #uuid "13697faa-c07f-521a-ba76-9771a21a0d13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ebd984-f944-5735-935d-58b8e1d12c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506683} {:answer/id #uuid "3faecea9-9832-54b2-afe3-75ade1b40121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506827} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3d8d44-88a4-5931-b74f-49ee9adea60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506953} {:answer/id #uuid "c047a9a1-43b8-5862-adef-5be69f583fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507100} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e8196-e4ed-521f-b41a-81344a83bfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507135} :answer-field/id #uuid "c755ac45-18f2-5688-96b0-fcf97a72209f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b3df8bd-2ae3-52a2-905f-ded2bd184071" :question/math-academy-id "q-48459" :question/problem "![](images/72/72e1d1f1ea1aa635441da5503a49ac4d33d5ab0df62d48aa699debc7816df1c2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h}=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=-0.5$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h}=1$" :question/worked-solution "Comparing these limits with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(-2), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(0), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function consists of three line segments. Inspecting the graph, we see that\n\n- for $x<-1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=1,$\n\n- for $-1<x<1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n- for $x>1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=1.$\n\nThe derivative does not exist at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ . So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h} & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only I and III are true."} :step/id #uuid "d8a08a0e-66bf-57d5-87e6-30f1a5ddd9cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6a23679-6599-5401-aade-8321affdc088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507184} :answer-field/id #uuid "101d9dd4-dd5a-5b40-bb06-cc4f4eee42ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "453b5d73-89c7-511f-b2be-1d3d4f762507" :question/math-academy-id "q-339039" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{f(x)-f(5)}{x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{5}^{2}}{x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(5).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{f}^{′}(5)=2\\cdot 5=10,\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=10.$"} :step/id #uuid "fcabb837-cb68-532d-838f-39c9a0d91ee1"}] :activity/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506668}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041} :activity/id #uuid "5c37473f-3f15-50ad-88ea-ddb970802b55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050756 :topic/id #uuid "642d830f-d131-58c7-ae56-060749a40776" :topic/math-academy-id 3811} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bc1975-006b-5be4-ad5c-88888abb00d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "003ba853-1e82-584c-b1f2-becf3ff9c30c" :step/math-academy-id 39810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183921 :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050071}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504648}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "e4ed7c3e-7162-558c-a918-39f63652e0c0" :step/math-academy-id 39811 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183922 :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504689}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "9666a609-3291-5b2c-a5f1-4c905924ddd1" :step/math-academy-id 39814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "24cdcba9-d6c5-5719-a086-34fe417cc42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6600 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Function at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "66882814-9d8d-500a-b199-d60870f60cec" :step/math-academy-id 39815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183923 :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504684}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"} :step/id #uuid "f8205256-4d92-59b5-babd-ec4ae58b06b8" :step/math-academy-id 39823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185362 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "926b00b5-d0b1-559a-9b04-c0c0636824d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6741 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "338537ec-9b29-534a-bc92-7f0375a54a6d" :step/math-academy-id 40843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185363 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eea2d2ac-ea9c-551a-91c6-4ee85ab05907" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6742 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "13c5d606-b95e-5a64-ba04-2a68fbf50e14" :step/math-academy-id 40844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051}}] :activity/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184398}]}
